llision cross sections  the arrival time for ions are recorded  along with their mass spectra to give us  a two dimensional plot of mobility and  mro z  55 iron packers are collected  within these two minutes of flow  injection runs this gives us roughly 49  packets of data for the 30 seconds of  the flow injection  this is a comparison of helic ms with  slim  ims ms analysis of released glycans  we can see that a typical helicon takes  15 minutes to separate glycans whereas  ims  can separate glycans in less than  seconds of runtime  the table uh  shows that many more glycan species are  detected using  two minutes flow injection run in  comparison to the 15 minutes of electron  times  we detected glycans that have a wide  range of mro z's and drift times if we  zoom into the most  relevant glycans we detect isomeric  glycans for g0f g1f and the scilated g1f  which is g1fs  here is an overall view of the glycan  distributions for both lupus and healthy  subjects  we also have a two global internal  standards from cool glycans for healthy  and lupus samples we collected  which are labeled as control 1 and  control 2.  it is known that galactose levels are  implicated in autoimmune disorders  low galactose levels have been reported  for rheumatoid  arthritis by us and others in previous  studies  however glycans derived from auto  antibodies in our analysis for lupus  does not show a statistically  significant lowering of galactose they  when we examine the intensity ratios  between g1f and g0f that is a measure of  a single galactose or intensity ratios  between g2f and g0f which is a measure  of two galactose units  we next looked at additional isomers  observed by sim separations for example  g1f glycan is resolved into three  reproducible peaks  this was performed in triplicate  measurements for all samples that were  analyzed  we used the intensities of the three g1f  isomeric species as  separately as predictors to categorize  lupus and healthy subjects  to our surprise rock curves are the  third g1f glycan  species is more predictive of the  disease state compared to the first and  second g1f glycan isomers  in the third case study we are using  slim separations to speed up  characterization of antibody drug  conjugates or adcs  an important parameter of evaluating the  conjugation of a small molecule drug to  the antibody is drug to antibody ratio  or da value  traditionally we have obtained r values  using time consuming 2d lcms analysis  here we are using size exclusion  chromatography separation in the first  dimension followed by reverse phase  separation in the second dimension  using multiple hard cutting two  dimensional lcs to obtain data values  by the ms signal  in this analysis we observe that the sec  retention time correlates very well with  the da value  alternative to this we can speed up the  characterization use using slim  slim adx  slim adc experiments were performed in a  johnson collaboration with the pacific  northwest national lab  in this experiment we were able to  rapidly and simultaneously identify  conjugated species as is and estimate  the dal values accurately for both the  heavy and light chains of a model  antibody drug conjugate  these da values were obtained in  significantly less time compared to size  exclusion chromatography  of the intact antibody and we can also  see that the da values compare quite  well with the sccms  multiplexing  multiplex sample analysis is another  important method to increase throughput  in biologics characterization  isobaric labeling reagents such as  standard mass tags or tmt for short and  other reagents have been used widely in  proteomics for relative and absolute  quantification  however their use in pharmaceutical  characterisation is rather limited  here we are using tandem mass tags to  create isobaric label peptides to  estimate the occupancy of a small  molecule drug conjugated to antibody  as shown in the diagram the workflow  starts with labeling the unconjugated  cysteine site of a protein of the adc  sample  and the same antibody without the drug  we call a mock sample  is then labeled with a first pair of  cysteine reactive tnt reagents or iodo  tmts  these parallel samples are then  trypsinized and the resulting peptides  are labeled with a second tmt tag  that are reactive to the n-terminus and  the license of the peptides  the peptides are pooled and then mixed  in equimolar ratios and analyzed by lcms  the peptides when subjected to ms2  generate these tnt report ions as shown  in the spectrum  for both the first and the second pairs  of tmp labels from which the occupancy  of the drag conjugation can be derived  as shown in the equation below  here are a few examples of site-specific  occupancies estimated persisting  conjugate  different sites have different  occupancies as seen in the top panel  the spectra show the reporters and the  bottom panel shows the corresponding  fragment eyes that localize the  conjugation size  once we obtain the occupancies for each  site we make use of the non-system tmp  ratios of the spectra which have  exclusively the second tmp label to  obtain a normalized section  factor between the conjugate and the  mock sample  we then use this factor to correct  occupancy  estimates for each site specific  existing conjugation site  using this approach we can multiplex up  to four different conjugation reactions  to estimate occupancies of a drug in a  single experiment  the experiment shown here is a  site-specific occupancy estimation using  tnt templates reagents to monitor the  conjugation of four different  conjugation reactions in a single run  with unprecedented throughput  our final approach to speed up biologics  characterization is rapid sample  introduction with no separations  this type of analysis is particularly  important for multi-specific antibodies  that can be introduced  to desired heterodimers as well as  undesirable homodynes during single cell  cloning  cell line development activities require  rapid product quality analysis  specifically looking at chain tearing of  multi-specifics  one needs to screen large number of  clones during cell line development  stages to identify and rank order cell  lines with the highest percentage of  these heterodivers  we are using rapid fire ms with cycle  times of 8 to 10 seconds per sample to  speed up this processing of large  volumes of samples  our approach starts with purified cell  culture material and subjecting samples  mostly in 96 well form to rapid fire msn  analysis  the data generated from these  experiments can be split to individual  files  and processed using a intact ms data  analysis pipeline  where intact mass spectra are  deconvoluted and intensities are  reported for each clone for various  species as shown in the heat map at the  bottom  here is an example where only 13 of the  high titer clones moved into cell line  selection due to high levels of  homodimers and half mass detected  through this type of analysis  in addition to chain misspelling we are  also exploring the use of rapid fire ms  for other applications  product quality attributes such as  glycostation can be estimated by  performing rapid fire ms analysis on  antibody fragments  in this example  we investigate the glycans of the heavy  chain located in the father and fc  domains using eyes  we can generate the fb and fc regions of  the antibody and analyze each individual  chain  for their glycan distribution  in addition lycos attenuation on  multi-specifics can be compared  by reduced mass analysis  the non-identical glycans on each heavy  chain can be rapidly analyzed using  rapid fire during product quality  screening  we have compared glycans between lcms  and rapid fire ms approach and find a  good correlation between the major  glycoforms  finally rapid fire ms can be used to  examine maps under native conditions  and here we are using charge reduction  to strip charges on a monoclonal  antibody to increase the distribution of  peaks that we believe  will help to improve charge  deconvolution  in this example we are increasing the  flow of the charge reducing reagent  introduced from the agilent reference  spray every fifth injection during the  rapid fire run  in conclusion i hope you have seen the  variety of methods we have developed to  accelerate biologics characterization in  a stage appropriate manner  these enabling technologies include  increasing digestion speeds several  million fold using micro droplets  as i pointed out micro droplets actually  accelerate otherwise very slow reactions  and is transformative in the  characterization of biologics with  increased throughput and versatility  next we examine short gradient lcs and  ion mobility separations to speed up  analysis time at unprecedented speeds  sample multiplexing for accurate and  fast quantitation was also this was also  demonstrated using tandem mass tag  approaches  and finally rapid sample introduction  from screening to product quality  assessment of molecules was  was demonstrated  uh what is the future our future is  looking bright we are continuously  innovating and improving our analytical  methods  informatics capabilities to become an  industry leader in biologics  characterization  we also see opportunities for  improvement in sample management and  data tracking systems  integrating high throughput mass  spectrometry with robotic sample  preparation  is one of the areas that we are looking  into  also increasing the information context  of our analysis by integrating various  assays  mass spectrometry assays with probably  biophysical assays in an  autonomous fashion so that we can  integrate results and deliver these  results to our stakeholders in an  integrated fashion  we admission technological innovation  will provide time to value in biologics  characterization  with that i would like to thank  all the color all the contributors at  jensen and our research partners and  collaborators finally i would like to  thank you for a participation in this  webinar and happy to answer any  questions thank you  thank you dr genordena for your  informative presentation we've received  some great questions already  our first question is  is micro micro droplet digestion being  performed on chemical reactions  uh it is um so this technique has been  pioneered by groups  both at purdue university and at the  at this at stanford  and both these groups uh professor cooks  and professors there have performed  these types of reactions  uh for organic molecules  and uh this uh  has a huge potential even for  pharmaceuticals biologics where  certain biologics need to be  modified  one such example which i've shown is  antibody drug conjugates and various  types of chemical modifications like  tmpp  and these are some of these uh chemicals  that we can use to  modify the proteins uh antibodies and  antigens  and these are some areas that we are  exploring in the future  okay great thank you i have another  question  is the multiplex experiment  uh in the multiplex experiment what is  the use of the second tmt tag  yeah that's excellent question uh so  this multiplex experiment uh  is a little different to the typical  proteomics experiment where researchers  typically use one type of labeling  reagent either it's a  lysine or  lysine or primary amine reactive  reagents or cysteine reacting reagents  uh it has never been used uh together uh  in a single experiment so the idea  behind this type of design is to use the  second label to do normalization  especially when we are looking into  conjugates in this particular experiment  we are trying to determine occupancy  the two work streams parallel work  streams can  can  needs to be um  the the protein amounts in the two  parallel workflows need to be the same  similar so in order to account for any  um  errors introduced during experiments  such as mixing errors  and  reaction errors we use we have to  correct for for the differences so we do  that by uh using the second reagent  which typically reacts with the  n-terminus or a lysine on the peptide  that we can use to normalize the ratios  and correct for any  errors that were introduced during  sample preparation  okay thank you for that  so far we have one more question  in the clipping experiment  are there any tags you can use for  detecting c-terminus clip sites  yeah excellent question again  so far uh the the the  the reagents that are available um  chemically is to react with the  interminus there's no reagent that can  efficiently tag at the c terminus  however  as i pointed out clipping is pretty  important attribute in biotherapeutics  characterization not all peptides that  we measure during such experiments  create  peptides that are sufficiently long to  be detected only at the end terminus so  there needs to be  age reagents that can also attach to the  c terminus  although there aren't  any reagents that are available  in the market at least for now  the c-terminus there's some research  groups especially in the organ  organic synthetic  organic synthesis where  people are trying to develop uh  c-terminal probes uh  bioconjugation probes by using  photochemical reactions  so these are still research interests as  they're still in research and not  commercially available but that is  something that  we would like to see happen and made  available to the uh by the broader  community balance the community so that  we can use this for biologics  characterization  right well thank you so much as a  reminder to our audience questions we  did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed via the email address  you provided at the time of the  registration  thank you again to our speaker harsha  gunawardena phd and to everyone who  participated in this presentation we  hope you enjoy the rest of the event
 

